      <title>France’s dilemma: protesters demand spending as markets require fiscal restraint</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/10/france-caught-between-bond-markets-student-worker-blockades</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Waves of protests expose debt crisis faced by French government, but political wrangling is stalling an escape plan. Is there a way out?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Eight years have passed since rising fuel prices brought the &lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/dec/03/who-are-the-gilets-jaunes-and-what-do-they-want"&gt;gilets &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/dec/03/who-are-the-gilets-jaunes-and-what-do-they-want"&gt;jaunes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt; (yellow vest) demonstrators to the streets of France, prompting the first big crisis of Emmanuel Macron’s time in the Élysée Palace.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As the French president contemplates his exit next year, turmoil has again gripped the streets in the &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/08/france-student-protests-schools-vow-to-continue"&gt;form of student protests&lt;/a&gt;, but this time the financial markets are unhappy too.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/10/france-caught-between-bond-markets-student-worker-blockades"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
